## 5. BasicMqttClientAuthProvider
**Path:** `/workspace/repo/application/src/main/java/org/thingsboard/mqtt/broker/service/auth/providers/BasicMqttClientAuthProvider.java`
**Package:** `org.thingsboard.mqtt.broker.service.auth.providers`

`@Service @RequiredArgsConstructor @Slf4j`, implements `MqttClientAuthProvider`.
- **Fields:** final `AuthorizationRuleService`, `MqttClientCredentialsService clientCredentialsService`, `CacheNameResolver cacheNameResolver`; non-final `BCryptPasswordEncoder passwordEncoder`, `HashFunction hashFunction`.
- **Constructor:** explicit `@Autowired` constructor (co-exists with `@RequiredArgsConstructor`) that takes the three services plus `@Lazy BCryptPasswordEncoder`, and initializes `hashFunction = Hashing.sha256()`.
- **Key methods:** `authenticate(AuthContext)`; private `authWithBasicCredentials(...)` (this is the cache-backed path), `getCredentialIds(...)`, `isMatchingPassword(...)`, `passwordBytesToString(...)`, `toHashString(...)`, and `getBasicCredsPwCache()`.
- **Caching:** uses `cacheNameResolver.getCache(CacheConstants.BASIC_CREDENTIALS_PASSWORD_CACHE)`, keyed by SHA-256 hash of the password, storing/reading `MqttClientCredentials`.

## Caching setup conventions

The project uses Spring's `CacheManager`/`Cache` abstraction with two swappable backends selected by the `cache.type` property, both defined in the `common/cache` module (`org.thingsboard.mqtt.broker.cache`):

- **`/workspace/repo/common/cache/src/main/java/org/thingsboard/mqtt/broker/cache/TbCaffeineCacheConfiguration.java`** — `@Configuration @ConditionalOnProperty(prefix="cache", value="type", havingValue="caffeine", matchIfMissing=true) @EnableCaching`. Its `@Bean CacheManager cacheManager()` builds a `SimpleCacheManager` of `CaffeineCache`s from a `CacheSpecsMap`. This is the default.
- **`/workspace/repo/common/cache/src/main/java/org/thingsboard/mqtt/broker/cache/TBRedisCacheConfiguration.java`** — abstract `@Configuration @ConditionalOnProperty(... havingValue="redis")`, `@Bean CacheManager cacheManager(RedisConnectionFactory cf)` returns a `RedisCacheManager` (transaction-aware).

**Consumption convention:** application/dao code never injects `CacheManager` directly. Instead they inject the `CacheNameResolver` component and call `cacheNameResolver.getCache(CacheConstants.SOME_CACHE)` to obtain a Spring `Cache`, then use `cache.get(key, Type.class)` / `cache.put(...)` / `cache.putIfAbsent(...)`. Cache names always come from `CacheConstants`.

Representative example — `/workspace/repo/dao/src/main/java/org/thingsboard/mqtt/broker/dao/client/device/DevicePacketIdAndSerialNumberServiceImpl.java`:
```java
private final CacheNameResolver cacheNameResolver;
...
private Cache getCache() {
    return cacheNameResolver.getCache(CacheConstants.PACKET_ID_AND_SERIAL_NUMBER_CACHE);
}
// usage: cache.get(clientId, PacketIdAndSerialNumber.class); cache.put(...); cache.putIfAbsent(...)
```

